Let $S = ([0,1], *, \le)$ be a linearly ordered semigroup whose underlying order is the usual order on $[0,1]$, such that:
(i) $*$ is associative and order-preserving in each argument;
(ii) $0$ is an annihilator, i.e. $0 * x = x * 0 = 0$ for every $x \in [0,1]$;
(iii) $S$ has no neutral element;
(iv) $*$ is continuous as a map $[0,1]^2 \to [0,1]$ (continuous case);
(v) $S$ is Archimedean: for every $x, y \in (0,1)$ there exists $n \in \mathbb{N}$ such that $x^n := x * x * \cdots * x$ ($n$ factors) satisfies $x^n < y$;
(vi) $S$ is nilpotent: for every $x \in [0,1)$ there exists $n \in \mathbb{N}$ with $x^n = 0$.

A non-decreasing mapping $f : [0,1] \to \mathbb{R}$ is called a multiplicative generator of $S$ if
$$x * y = f^{(-1)}\bigl(f(x) \cdot f(y)\bigr) \qquad \text{for all } x, y \in [0,1],$$
where $f^{(-1)}$ denotes the pseudo-inverse of $f$ (defined, for $u$ in the range of $f$, as a value $v \in [0,1]$ with $f(v) = u$, and extended in the standard non-decreasing manner outside the range) and $\cdot$ is the ordinary multiplication of real numbers.

Characterize precisely those nilpotent interval semigroups $S$ (in the above sense) that admit a continuous multiplicative generator $f : [0,1] \to \mathbb{R}$.

